<?php
return [
    1 => [
        'title' => 'Obsessions vs. Compulsions & The OCRD Diagnostic Realm',
        'slug' => 'obsessions-compulsions-ocrd-realm',
        'description' => 'Ego-dystonicity, the anxiety-neutralization loop, creation of the dedicated OCRD chapter in DSM-5, and core definitions.',
        'overview' => "In DSM-5, Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders (OCRD) were officially removed from the Anxiety Disorders chapter and established as an independent, standalone diagnostic chapter. This fundamental taxonomic restructuring was grounded in decades of converging neuroimaging, genetic, and pharmacological data demonstrating that obsessive-compulsive pathology is driven by frontostriatal habit-loop dysregulation and repetitive behavioral compulsivity, distinguishing it from the amygdala-mediated fear circuits that define pure anxiety disorders.\n\nThe clinical hallmark of Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD) is the cyclic interaction between obsessions and compulsions. Obsessions are recurrent, persistent, intrusive, and unwanted thoughts, urges, or images that cause marked anxiety or distress. Crucially, obsessions are experienced as ego-dystonic—meaning they feel alien, involuntary, and repugnant to the individual's core values, morality, and self-concept (e.g., a devoted parent experiencing intrusive blasphemous or violent images).\n\nCompulsions are repetitive behaviors (e.g., hand washing, ordering, checking) or mental acts (e.g., praying, counting, repeating words silently) that an individual feels driven to perform in response to an obsession or according to rigid rules. Compulsions serve to neutralize the distress caused by the obsession or prevent a dreaded catastrophe; however, they are either clearly excessive or not connected in a realistic way with what they are designed to prevent.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A requires the presence of obsessions, compulsions, or both:\n\nObsessions are defined by (1) and (2):\n1. Recurrent and persistent thoughts, urges, or images that are experienced, at some time during the disturbance, as intrusive and unwanted, and that in most individuals cause marked anxiety or distress.\n2. The individual attempts to ignore or suppress such thoughts, urges, or images, or to neutralize them with some other thought or action (i.e., by performing a compulsion).\n\nCompulsions are defined by (1) and (2):\n1. Repetitive behaviors (e.g., hand washing, ordering, checking) or mental acts (e.g., praying, counting, repeating words silently) that the individual feels driven to perform in response to an obsession or according to rules that must be applied rigidly.\n2. The behaviors or mental acts are aimed at preventing or reducing anxiety or distress, or preventing some dreaded event or situation; however, these behaviors or mental acts either are not connected in a realistic way with what they are designed to neutralize or prevent, or are clearly excessive.\n\nCriterion B establishes the clinical threshold: The obsessions or compulsions are time-consuming (e.g., take more than 1 hour per day) or cause clinically significant distress or functional impairment (social, occupational).\n\nCriterion C and D require ruling out physiological effects of substances, medical conditions, and other mental disorders.",
        'specifiers' => "Every OCRD diagnosis in DSM-5 mandates the assignment of an explicit Insight Specifier: 'With good or fair insight' (recognizes beliefs are definitely or probably not true), 'With poor insight' (thinks beliefs are probably true), or 'With absent insight/delusional beliefs' (completely convinced beliefs are true).\n\nAdditionally, OCD includes the 'Tic-related' specifier: assigned if the individual has a current or past history of a tic disorder.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "OCD vs. Generalized Anxiety Disorder (GAD): GAD worries focus on real-life concerns (finances, health, job) and are experienced as ego-syntonic concerns, lacking motor compulsions; OCD obsessions are intrusive, bizarre, ego-dystonic themes neutralized by rigid rituals.\n\nOCD vs. Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder (OCPD): OCPD is a pervasive personality style characterized by perfectionism, orderliness, and control that is ego-syntonic (the person values their high standards); OCD is ego-dystonic and driven by true obsessions and compulsions.\n\nOCD with Absent Insight vs. Schizophrenia: In OCD with absent insight, the delusional belief is embedded within repetitive compulsive neutralizing rituals; in schizophrenia, delusions lack compulsive neutralizing rituals and feature formal thought disorder.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 26-year-old software engineer who experiences intrusive, terrifying thoughts that touching door handles will contaminate their hands with deadly toxins and kill their family members (ego-dystonic obsession).\n\nTo neutralize the terror, the engineer washes their hands in boiling water according to a rigid 15-minute counting ritual, repeating the sequence 12 times a day (compulsion taking over 3 hours daily). The engineer recognizes that the fear is irrational (good insight). The presence of intrusive obsessions neutralized by repetitive compulsions consuming >1 hour daily confirms Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder, With good or fair insight.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: OCD was moved out of Anxiety Disorders into its own dedicated chapter in DSM-5. Obsessions are intrusive, ego-dystonic thoughts/images; compulsions are repetitive behaviors/mental acts performed to neutralize anxiety. Symptoms must be time-consuming (taking > 1 hour per day) or cause marked functional impairment. DSM-5 mandates an Insight Specifier (Good/Fair, Poor, Absent/Delusional). Up to 30% of OCD patients have a lifetime tic disorder (Tic-related specifier).",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The Dedicated OCRD Chapter', 'icon' => '🏛️', 'definition' => 'DSM-5 established OCRD as a separate chapter, recognizing frontostriatal compulsivity over pure anxiety fear circuits.'],
            ['title' => 'Ego-Dystonic Intrusiveness', 'icon' => '⚡', 'definition' => 'Obsessions are experienced as unacceptable, alien, and contradictory to the individual\'s core moral identity.'],
            ['title' => 'The Neutralization Imperative', 'icon' => '🔄', 'definition' => 'Compulsions are performed to temporarily reduce anxiety or magically prevent a dreaded catastrophic outcome.'],
            ['title' => 'The 1-Hour Time-Consuming Rule', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'Diagnostic threshold requires symptoms to occupy more than 1 hour per day or cause significant functional impairment.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Ego-Dystonic', 'definition' => 'Thoughts, impulses, or behaviors that are unacceptable, distressing, and inconsistent with the fundamental self-concept and values of the individual.'],
            ['term' => 'Mental Compulsion', 'definition' => 'An internal cognitive ritual (silent prayer, counting, repeating specific phrases) performed covertly to neutralize an intrusive obsession.'],
            ['term' => 'Thought-Action Fusion', 'definition' => 'A cognitive distortion common in OCD wherein an individual believes having a bad thought is morally equivalent to performing the action, or makes the event more likely to occur.'],
            ['term' => 'Magical Thinking', 'definition' => 'The erroneous belief that one\'s thoughts, words, or idiosyncratic compulsive rituals can physically cause or prevent external events without a causal mechanism.']
        ]
    ],
    2 => [
        'title' => 'Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD: Contamination, Symmetry, Harm)',
        'slug' => 'ocd-symptom-dimensions-subtypes',
        'description' => 'The four core symptom dimensions: Contamination/Cleaning, Symmetry/Ordering, Taboo/Unacceptable thoughts, and Harm/Checking.',
        'overview' => "While Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD) is classified as a single diagnostic entity in DSM-5, decades of factor-analytic psychiatric research confirm that OCD symptoms cluster into four distinct, cross-culturally validated symptom dimensions: (1) Contamination obsessions with cleaning/washing compulsions; (2) Symmetry obsessions with ordering, arranging, and counting compulsions; (3) Taboo or unacceptable thoughts (aggressive, sexual, religious/scrupulosity) with neutralizing mental compulsions; and (4) Harm obsessions (fears of causing harm to self or others) with checking compulsions.\n\nThese dimensions are not mutually exclusive—patients frequently experience symptoms across multiple dimensions over their lifetime—yet they exhibit distinct biological correlates and treatment responses. For example, the Symmetry/Ordering dimension is strongly associated with early childhood onset, male predominance, and comorbid tic disorders, whereas Contamination/Washing features higher female representation and responds well to standard Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP).\n\nUnderstanding these dimensional presentations is critical for clinical assessment. Patients with taboo thoughts (e.g., intrusive pedophilic, violent, or blasphemous images) frequently suffer in intense secrecy for years due to profound shame, fearing they are dangerous criminals rather than recognizing their thoughts as classic ego-dystonic manifestations of OCD.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Diagnostic evaluation requires mapping the patient's symptoms onto the established OCD dimensions:\n\n1. Contamination / Washing: Preoccupation with germs, bodily fluids, dirt, chemicals, or environmental contaminants, paired with excessive washing, showering, or cleaning rituals, and avoidance of perceived contaminants.\n\n2. Symmetry / Ordering / Counting: Preoccupation with exactness, alignment, symmetry, or balance, paired with compulsive ordering, arranging, repeating actions until they feel 'just right', or counting in specific numerical patterns.\n\n3. Taboo Thoughts / Neutralization: Intrusive unacceptable images or impulses of a sexual, violent, or religious nature (scrupulosity), paired with covert mental rituals, silent prayers, or reassurance seeking.\n\n4. Doubt / Harm / Checking: Persistent pathological doubt that one has caused or will cause harm (e.g., hitting a pedestrian with a car, leaving the stove on, leaving doors unlocked), paired with repetitive checking rituals and retracing steps.",
        'specifiers' => "Every diagnosis of OCD must include: (1) The Insight Specifier ('With good or fair insight', 'With poor insight', or 'With absent insight/delusional beliefs'); and (2) The Tic Specifier ('Tic-related' if there is a current or past history of a tic disorder).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "OCD Taboo Violent Thoughts vs. True Psychopathic Intent: Individuals with OCD are horrified by their intrusive violent thoughts, actively resist them, and engage in elaborate rituals to prevent harm; individuals with conduct disorder or antisocial personality find aggressive thoughts ego-syntonic and act on them without remorse.\n\nContamination OCD vs. Delusional Disorder, Somatic Type: In OCD, the patient engages in washing rituals and typically retains some doubt regarding contamination; in Delusional Disorder, the conviction is fixed, unshakeable, and lacks compulsive cleaning rituals.\n\nSymmetry OCD vs. Autism Spectrum Disorder: In ASD, repetitive ordering is pleasurable or comforting (part of restricted interests); in OCD, ordering is driven by anxiety reduction and avoidance of catastrophe or uncomfortable tension.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 20-year-old student with a past history of childhood motor tics who presents with severe distress. When writing or arranging books on a desk, the student experiences intense somatic tension unless items are aligned in multiples of four with millimeter precision until it feels 'just right'.\n\nIf interrupted, the student experiences overwhelming distress and must restart the entire 45-minute ordering sequence. The student spends four hours daily arranging items. The presentation satisfies the Symmetry/Ordering dimension of OCD (>1 hour/day, marked impairment), and the documented history of childhood tics warrants the Tic-Related specifier: Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder, With good or fair insight, Tic-related.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: The 4 major symptom dimensions of OCD are: (1) Contamination/Cleaning, (2) Symmetry/Ordering, (3) Taboo thoughts/Neutralizing, and (4) Harm/Checking. Symmetry/Ordering is strongly linked to comorbid Tic Disorders and male predominance. The 'just right' phenomenon is a sensory-affective tension driving symmetry rituals. Taboo aggressive/sexual thoughts in OCD are strictly ego-dystonic; patients do not act on them. First-line psychotherapy is Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP).",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Four Dimensional Clusters', 'icon' => '🧩', 'definition' => 'Contamination/Cleaning, Symmetry/Ordering, Taboo/Unacceptable thoughts, and Harm/Checking.'],
            ['title' => 'The Just Right Phenomenon', 'icon' => '📐', 'definition' => 'An uncomfortable internal somatosensory tension driving compulsive symmetry and ordering until an exact sensation is achieved.'],
            ['title' => 'Ego-Dystonic Taboo Thoughts', 'icon' => '🛡️', 'definition' => 'Intrusive blasphemous, violent, or sexual images that are abhorrent to the patient, presenting zero risk of violent enactment.'],
            ['title' => 'Tic-Related Specifier Biology', 'icon' => '⚡', 'definition' => 'Tic-related OCD involves higher genetic loading, basal ganglia dopaminergic involvement, and earlier childhood onset.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP)', 'definition' => 'The gold-standard cognitive-behavioral therapy for OCD, involving systematic exposure to feared obsessional triggers while refraining from compulsive rituals.'],
            ['term' => 'Scrupulosity', 'definition' => 'A manifestation of OCD involving pathological guilt, moral obsessions, and compulsive religious rituals over perceived blasphemy or sin.'],
            ['term' => 'Sensory-Affective Tension', 'definition' => 'An internal visceral discomfort (the \'incomplete\' or \'not just right\' feeling) that precedes compulsions in symmetry-predominant OCD.'],
            ['term' => 'Reassurance Seeking', 'definition' => 'An interpersonal compulsive behavior wherein an individual repeatedly asks others to verify that no harm has occurred or will occur.']
        ]
    ],
    3 => [
        'title' => 'Body Dysmorphic Disorder (BDD & Muscle Dysmorphia)',
        'slug' => 'body-dysmorphic-disorder-muscle-dysmorphia',
        'description' => 'Preoccupation with perceived physical defects (invisible to others), repetitive mirror checking/grooming, and the muscle dysmorphia specifier.',
        'overview' => "Body Dysmorphic Disorder (BDD) was relocated in DSM-5 from Somatoform Disorders to the Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders chapter. BDD is characterized by a persistent, distressing preoccupation with one or more perceived defects or flaws in physical appearance that are either entirely unnoticeable or appear only slight to others. The preoccupation drives repetitive behavioral rituals (such as mirror checking, excessive grooming, skin picking, or seeking reassurance) or mental acts (comparing appearance to others) that consume hours each day.\n\nThe most common bodily areas of preoccupation are the skin (acne, scars, wrinkles, complexion), hair (thinning, facial hair), and nose. Crucially, the physical flaw is objective imperceptible or trivial; BDD is not ordinary cosmetic vanity. Patients experience profound shame, social anxiety, and occupational collapse, frequently becoming housebound. BDD carries alarmingly high rates of suicidal ideation (up to 80%) and completed suicide attempts (estimated at 25%), reflecting pervasive demoralization.\n\nA central clinical feature of BDD is its poor response to cosmetic and dermatological procedures. Individuals frequently seek repeated plastic surgeries, dermatological peels, or dental procedures; however, procedural interventions almost never resolve the underlying neurobiological perceptual distortion, frequently worsening dissatisfaction and leading to legal disputes or violence against clinicians.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A requires preoccupation with one or more perceived defects or flaws in physical appearance that are not observable or appear only slight to others.\n\nCriterion B mandates that at some point during the course of the disorder, the individual has performed repetitive behaviors (e.g., mirror checking, excessive grooming, skin picking, reassurance seeking) or mental acts (e.g., comparing his or her appearance with that of others) in response to the appearance concerns.\n\nCriterion C establishes that the preoccupation causes clinically significant distress or impairment in social, occupational, or other important areas of functioning.\n\nCriterion D mandates that the appearance preoccupation is not better explained by concerns with body fat or weight in an individual whose symptoms meet diagnostic criteria for an eating disorder (e.g., Anorexia Nervosa).",
        'specifiers' => "Specifier: 'With muscle dysmorphia' (assigned if the individual is preoccupied with the idea that his or her body build is too small or insufficiently muscular; occurs almost exclusively in males and is strongly associated with anabolic steroid abuse).\n\nMandatory Insight Specifier: 'With good or fair insight', 'With poor insight', or 'With absent insight/delusional beliefs' (completely convinced that the perceived defect is catastrophic and noticeable to all). Notably, absent insight/delusional beliefs occurs in up to 30% of BDD patients.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "BDD vs. Anorexia Nervosa: In Anorexia Nervosa, the preoccupation is focused on overall body weight, shape, and fat; if the preoccupation is limited to weight/fat in an underweight individual, Anorexia Nervosa is diagnosed, NOT BDD. If the preoccupation targets discrete non-weight features (e.g., nose shape, facial skin), BDD can be co-diagnosed.\n\nBDD vs. Social Anxiety Disorder: While both involve social avoidance, Social Anxiety involves fear of negative evaluation regarding behavior or speech, whereas BDD avoidance is driven specifically by terror of being scrutinized for perceived physical ugliness.\n\nBDD vs. Normal Cosmetic Concerns: Normal appearance concerns do not consume hours per day, do not drive compulsive rituals, and do not cause functional disability.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 22-year-old male bodybuilder who lifts weights for four hours daily and consumes 5,000 calories while using illicit anabolic steroids. Despite having profound muscular hypertrophy, he refuses to wear short-sleeved shirts, asserting that his chest and arms are 'shriveled, weak, and pathetic' (perceived muscular deficiency).\n\nHe spends two hours daily examining his muscles in mirrors and comparing himself unfavorably to fitness models, and has quit his job to avoid being seen in public. Meeting criteria for appearance preoccupation with repetitive checking rituals and social impairment, the clinical formulation confirms Body Dysmorphic Disorder, With Muscle Dysmorphia, With poor insight.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: BDD requires preoccupation with perceived physical flaws that are NOT observable or only slight to others, driving repetitive behaviors (mirror checking, grooming) or mental acts. Muscle dysmorphia is a specifier for BDD (belief that body build is too small/insufficiently muscular). Cosmetic surgery does NOT treat BDD and is contraindicated. Anorexia Nervosa takes precedence if preoccupation is limited to body weight/fat. BDD carries exceptionally high suicide rates (25% attempt rate).",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Perceived Imperceptible Flaw', 'icon' => '🪞', 'definition' => 'Preoccupation targets flaws that are completely unnoticeable or appear trivial to objective observers.'],
            ['title' => 'Repetitive Behavioral Enactment', 'icon' => '🔄', 'definition' => 'Mandatory performance of repetitive rituals: mirror checking, camouflage, skin picking, or social comparison.'],
            ['title' => 'The Muscle Dysmorphia Specifier', 'icon' => '💪', 'definition' => 'Preoccupation with insufficient muscularity, strongly linked to anabolic steroid misuse and male presentation.'],
            ['title' => 'Cosmetic Surgery Inefficacy', 'icon' => '🚫', 'definition' => 'Dermatological and plastic surgical procedures fail to resolve BDD and frequently escalate obsessional dissatisfaction.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Muscle Dysmorphia (Bigorexia)', 'definition' => 'A subtype of Body Dysmorphic Disorder marked by an unshakeable conviction that one\'s body build is abnormally small, lean, or insufficiently muscular.'],
            ['term' => 'Camouflaging', 'definition' => 'Compulsive behavioral strategies (heavy makeup, hats, baggy clothing, specific lighting) used to conceal perceived bodily defects from public scrutiny.'],
            ['term' => 'Absent Insight / Delusional BDD', 'definition' => 'A severe presentation where the patient is 100% convinced that their perceived flaw is hideous and that others mock them for it.'],
            ['term' => 'Anabolic-Androgenic Steroids (AAS)', 'definition' => 'Synthetic testosterone derivatives frequently abused by individuals with muscle dysmorphia to achieve extreme muscular growth.']
        ]
    ],
    4 => [
        'title' => 'Hoarding Disorder: Difficulty Discarding & Living Space Clutter',
        'slug' => 'hoarding-disorder-criteria-clutter',
        'description' => 'Persistent difficulty discarding, perceived need to save items, compromise of active living spaces, and excessive acquisition specifier.',
        'overview' => "Hoarding Disorder was introduced as a new, standalone diagnostic entity in DSM-5. In DSM-IV, hoarding was considered merely an optional symptom of Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder or a severe subtype of OCD. Extensive empirical clinical research demonstrated that the overwhelming majority of individuals who engage in severe hoarding do not experience classic OCD obsessions or motor compulsions. Furthermore, hoarding exhibits distinct genetic markers, neuroimaging patterns (anterior cingulate and insula hypo/hyperactivation during discarding tasks), and poor responsiveness to standard OCD pharmacological treatments.\n\nThe core psychopathology of Hoarding Disorder is defined by persistent difficulty discarding or parting with possessions, regardless of their actual value. This difficulty stems from a perceived need to save the items and the intense distress associated with the prospect of discarding them. Consequently, possessions accumulate to such an extent that active living spaces (kitchens, bedrooms, bathrooms) become compromised and can no longer be used for their intended purposes.\n\nHoarding behavior creates severe public health, sanitary, and safety hazards, including fire risks, structural collapse, and rodent infestations. Crucially, hoarding must be distinguished from normal collecting: normal collectors acquire items in a selective, organized, and intentional manner, maintain items without compromising living spaces, and do not experience severe functional disability.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A requires persistent difficulty discarding or parting with possessions, regardless of their actual value.\n\nCriterion B mandates that this difficulty is due to a perceived need to save the items and to the distress associated with discarding them.\n\nCriterion C establishes the environmental hallmark: The difficulty discarding possessions results in the accumulation of possessions that congest and clutter active living areas and substantially compromises their intended use. If living areas are uncluttered, it is only because of the intervention of third parties (e.g., family members, cleaners, authorities).\n\nCriterion D requires that the hoarding causes clinically significant distress or impairment in social, occupational, or other important areas of functioning (including maintaining a safe environment for self and others).\n\nCriterion E dictates that the hoarding is not attributable to another medical condition (e.g., brain injury, cerebrovascular disease, Prader-Willi syndrome). Criterion F mandates that the hoarding is not better explained by symptoms of another mental disorder (e.g., obsessions in OCD, decreased energy in MDD, delusions in Schizophrenia, major neurocognitive disorder).",
        'specifiers' => "Specifier: 'With excessive acquisition' (assigned if difficulty discarding is accompanied by excessive acquisition of items that are not needed or for which there is no available space; present in approximately 80-90% of hoarding cases).\n\nMandatory Insight Specifier: 'With good or fair insight', 'With poor insight', or 'With absent insight/delusional beliefs' (the individual is completely convinced that the hoarding behaviors and clutter are not problematic despite severe hazards). Poor or absent insight is exceedingly common in hoarding.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Hoarding Disorder vs. OCD Hoarding: In OCD hoarding, collecting items is driven by true intrusive obsessions (e.g., fear of harm, contamination, or magical thinking) and is experienced as ego-dystonic; in Hoarding Disorder, saving items is experienced as ego-syntonic, driven by perceived utility, sentimental value, or aesthetic appreciation, and distress occurs ONLY when forced to discard.\n\nHoarding vs. Normal Collecting: Collectors acquire organized, cataloged items without cluttering active living spaces or creating health hazards.\n\nHoarding Due to Neurocognitive Disorder: Severe dementia can lead to disorganized accumulation; however, onset is late, and memory/executive decline is prominent.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 62-year-old retired clerk whose adult children contact adult protective services after discovering the parent's home. Stacks of old newspapers, broken appliances, cardboard boxes, and unused clothing fill all rooms from floor to ceiling.\n\nThe kitchen stove and sink cannot be reached, the bed is covered in clutter forcing the parent to sleep in a chair, and pathways between rooms are narrow tunnels. When children attempt to discard 10-year-old circulars, the parent experiences intense panic and rage, insisting the paper may be useful in the future. The compromise of active living spaces and severe difficulty discarding confirm Hoarding Disorder, With poor insight, With excessive acquisition.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Hoarding Disorder was established as a new standalone diagnosis in DSM-5 (removed from OCD/OCPD). Key criteria: persistent difficulty discarding items regardless of value, perceived need to save items, and compromise of active living spaces. 'With excessive acquisition' applies if excessive buying or gathering occurs (80-90% of cases). In Hoarding Disorder, saving is ego-syntonic (feels comforting); in OCD hoarding, saving is ego-dystonic and driven by intrusive obsessions. Poor insight is very common.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Standalone DSM-5 Classification', 'icon' => '🏛️', 'definition' => 'Established as an independent disorder separate from OCD, reflecting distinct genetics, neural circuits, and treatment pathways.'],
            ['title' => 'Compromise of Active Living Areas', 'icon' => '🏠', 'definition' => 'Clutter congests essential living spaces (kitchen, bedroom, bathroom) so they can no longer function for their intended purposes.'],
            ['title' => 'Ego-Syntonic Attachment', 'icon' => '📦', 'definition' => 'Saving items feels comforting, sentimental, or practical; emotional distress emerges only upon the prospect of discarding.'],
            ['title' => 'Excessive Acquisition Specifier', 'icon' => '🛒', 'definition' => 'Applied when hoarding behavior includes active, relentless purchasing or gathering of free items without space.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Clutter', 'definition' => 'A large group of usually unrelated or disorganized possessions that congest active living spaces and compromise their intended use.'],
            ['term' => 'Active Living Space', 'definition' => 'Rooms within a residence designated for essential daily activities (sleeping, cooking, bathing, walking) rather than storage spaces.'],
            ['term' => 'Sentimental Attachment', 'definition' => 'An exaggerated emotional bond to inanimate objects, treating possessions as extensions of personal identity or memories.'],
            ['term' => 'Animal Hoarding', 'definition' => 'A severe manifestation of hoarding disorder involving the accumulation of large numbers of animals without providing minimal standards of nutrition and veterinary care.']
        ]
    ],
    5 => [
        'title' => 'Body-Focused Repetitive Behaviors: Trichotillomania & Excoriation',
        'slug' => 'body-focused-repetitive-behaviors-trichotillomania-excoriation',
        'description' => 'Trichotillomania (hair-pulling) and Excoriation (skin-picking) diagnostic criteria, repeated attempts to decrease, and habit reversal therapy.',
        'overview' => "Body-Focused Repetitive Behaviors (BFRBs) in DSM-5 encompass Trichotillomania (Hair-Pulling Disorder) and Excoriation (Skin-Picking Disorder). Both conditions were consolidated into the Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders chapter, reflecting shared pathophysiology involving motor impulsivity, tactile self-soothing rituals, and altered frontostriatal reward-processing mechanisms.\n\nTrichotillomania is characterized by the recurrent pulling out of one's hair, resulting in noticeable hair loss, accompanied by repeated attempts to decrease or stop hair pulling. The most common pulling sites are the scalp, eyebrows, and eyelashes, though hair from any bodily region may be targeted. Hair pulling may occur in brief bursts or sustained sessions lasting hours, and is frequently preceded by increasing tension or boredom and followed by gratification, pleasure, or a sense of relief.\n\nExcoriation Disorder involves recurrent skin picking that results in cutaneous lesions, accompanied by repeated attempts to decrease or stop picking. The most common picking sites are the face, arms, and hands. A critical diagnostic update in DSM-5 was the elimination of the historical requirement that pulling or picking must be preceded by rising tension and followed by relief; contemporary research demonstrates that many individuals pull or pick automatically outside conscious awareness ('automatic' vs. 'focused' picking).",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "For Trichotillomania (Hair-Pulling Disorder):\nCriterion A requires recurrent pulling out of one's hair, resulting in hair loss.\nCriterion B mandates repeated attempts to decrease or stop hair pulling.\nCriterion C requires that the hair pulling causes clinically significant distress or impairment in social, occupational, or other important areas of functioning.\nCriterion D establishes that hair pulling is not attributable to another medical condition (e.g., alopecia areata, dermatological conditions).\nCriterion E dictates that hair pulling is not better explained by symptoms of another mental disorder (e.g., attempts to improve a perceived appearance flaw in Body Dysmorphic Disorder).\n\nFor Excoriation (Skin-Picking Disorder):\nCriterion A requires recurrent skin picking resulting in skin lesions.\nCriterion B mandates repeated attempts to decrease or stop skin picking.\nCriterion C requires clinically significant distress or functional impairment.\nCriterion D establishes that skin picking is not attributable to physiological effects of a substance (e.g., cocaine, amphetamines) or another medical condition (e.g., scabies).\nCriterion E dictates that skin picking is not better explained by another mental disorder (e.g., tactile hallucinations in a psychotic disorder, or appearance concerns in BDD).",
        'specifiers' => "Both disorders are coded independently within OCRD, documenting whether the behavior is predominantly 'focused' (performed with conscious awareness to relieve tension) or 'automatic' (performed unconsciously during sedentary activities like reading or watching television).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "BFRBs vs. Body Dysmorphic Disorder: In BDD, skin picking or hair manipulation is performed specifically to fix, smooth, or remove a perceived defect in appearance (e.g., picking at perceived blemishes); in Excoriation and Trichotillomania, the behavior is not driven by appearance preoccupations.\n\nExcoriation vs. Substance-Induced Skin Picking: Methamphetamine and cocaine intoxication cause intense tactile hallucinations ('formication' or 'crank bugs'), driving severe skin excoriation; toxicology screening and temporal history are mandatory.\n\nTrichotillomania vs. Alopecia Areata: Dermatological biopsy and pull tests distinguish autoimmune patchy alopecia from the broken, irregular hair shafts characteristic of trichotillomania.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 19-year-old student who presents with irregular patches of hair loss across the crown of the scalp. Examination reveals short, broken hairs of varying lengths without scalp inflammation. The student admits to pulling out hairs individually while studying in the evening, experiencing a soothing sensation upon extracting the hair bulb.\n\nThe student has bought hats to hide the bald spots and has made repeated failed attempts to stop pulling by wearing gloves. The student does not believe their hair looks defective (ruling out BDD). Meeting criteria for recurrent hair pulling with hair loss, repeated attempts to stop, and social distress, the diagnosis is confirmed as Trichotillomania (Hair-Pulling Disorder).",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Both Trichotillomania and Excoriation require recurrent pulling/picking resulting in hair loss/lesions AND repeated attempts to decrease or stop the behavior. DSM-5 eliminated the requirement of tension before and relief after pulling/picking. Rule out BDD: BDD skin picking is driven by perceived cosmetic flaws; Excoriation is not. Rule out stimulant/methamphetamine toxicity (formication). Habit Reversal Training (HRT) is the psychological treatment of choice.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Mandatory Cessation Attempt Rule', 'icon' => '🛑', 'definition' => 'Both disorders require documented repeated, unsuccessful attempts to decrease or stop the repetitive behavior.'],
            ['title' => 'Elimination of Tension-Relief Mandate', 'icon' => '🔄', 'definition' => 'DSM-5 removed the requirement that pulling/picking must be preceded by tension and followed by gratification.'],
            ['title' => 'Automatic vs. Focused Behavior', 'icon' => '🧠', 'definition' => 'Behaviors can occur automatically during sedentary focus or focused consciously to alleviate dysphoria.'],
            ['title' => 'Habit Reversal Training (HRT)', 'icon' => '🛠️', 'definition' => 'The gold-standard behavioral therapy utilizing awareness training, competing response training, and stimulus control.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Habit Reversal Training', 'definition' => 'A multi-component behavioral intervention that trains individuals to recognize pulling urges and engage in a physically incompatible competing response.'],
            ['term' => 'Trichophagia', 'definition' => 'The compulsive swallowing of pulled hair, which carries severe medical risks of developing gastric hairballs (trichobezoars).'],
            ['term' => 'Competing Response', 'definition' => 'A physically incompatible motor action (e.g., clenching fists, holding a ball) engaged in for several minutes to interrupt a repetitive picking/pulling cycle.'],
            ['term' => 'Formication', 'definition' => 'The tactile hallucination of insects crawling under the skin, classically driving drug-induced skin excoriation in stimulant toxicity.']
        ]
    ],
    6 => [
        'title' => 'Insight Specifiers (Good/Fair, Poor, Delusional) & Tic Subtypes',
        'slug' => 'insight-specifiers-tic-subtypes-ocrd',
        'description' => 'The three insight tiers (Good/Fair, Poor, Absent/Delusional), differential boundary with psychosis, and the Tic-related specifier.',
        'overview' => "A pivotal structural innovation in DSM-5 was the introduction of standardized, uniform Insight Specifiers across the entire Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders chapter. Historically in DSM-IV, an individual whose obsessive beliefs reached delusional intensity was categorized under Psychotic Disorders Not Otherwise Specified or Schizophrenia. DSM-5 revolutionized this approach by recognizing that insight exists along a continuous spectrum within OCRD itself, ranging from full rational awareness to absolute delusional conviction.\n\nUnder DSM-5, an individual with OCD who is 100% convinced that their obsessional fears are real (e.g., completely convinced that failure to wash hands will cause family death) is classified as having 'OCD, with absent insight/delusional beliefs', NOT a primary psychotic disorder. This critical distinction prevents inappropriate diagnostic reclassification and ensures that patients receive first-line high-dose serotonergic and exposure-based treatments rather than being mismanaged as schizophrenia.\n\nFurthermore, the 'Tic-related' specifier identifies a biologically distinct subtype of OCD affecting up to 30% of patients. Individuals with tic-related OCD typically exhibit earlier age of onset (childhood), male predominance, higher rates of symmetry and ordering symptoms, prominent sensory-affective 'just right' phenomena, and frequently require dopamine-modulating augmentation strategies.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Standardized Insight Specifiers applied across OCD, BDD, and Hoarding Disorder:\n\n1. With Good or Fair Insight: The individual recognizes that obsessive-compulsive beliefs are definitely or probably not true, or that they may or may not be true (e.g., acknowledges that house will probably not burn down if stove is not checked 30 times).\n\n2. With Poor Insight: The individual thinks that obsessive-compulsive beliefs are probably true (e.g., believes house will probably burn down if stove is not checked 30 times).\n\n3. With Absent Insight / Delusional Beliefs: The individual is completely convinced that obsessive-compulsive beliefs are true (e.g., 100% convinced house will definitely burn down if stove is not checked 30 times).\n\nTic Specifier Formulation:\n'Tic-related': Assigned if the individual has a current or past history of a tic disorder (Tourette's Disorder, Persistent Motor or Vocal Tic Disorder, or Provisional Tic Disorder).",
        'specifiers' => "Every diagnosed case of OCD, BDD, and Hoarding must be explicitly coded with one of the three insight specifiers. OCD must additionally be evaluated for the 'Tic-related' specifier.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "OCD with Absent Insight vs. Delusional Disorder: In OCD with absent insight, the delusional conviction is directly embedded within repetitive compulsive rituals (washing, checking) or taboo obsessions; in Delusional Disorder, delusions are circumscribed and lack compulsive motor rituals.\n\nOCD with Absent Insight vs. Schizophrenia: Schizophrenia features formal thought disorder (derailment, loose associations), hallucinations, negative symptoms, and gross functional decline across non-obsessional spheres.\n\nTic-Related OCD vs. Tourette's Motor Tics: Complex motor tics (e.g., touching objects, tapping) may resemble compulsions; however, tics are preceded by somatic premonitory urges and lack obsessional cognitive content, whereas compulsions are driven by neutralizing anxiety or magical thinking.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 21-year-old student who spends six hours per day checking locks, appliances, and windows. The student reports an unshakable, 100% conviction that an electrical fire will incinerate their family if the stove knob is not turned back and forth 44 times.\n\nThe student exhibits no auditory hallucinations, no disorganized speech, and intact thinking in all academic domains outside the home safety sphere. Because the belief is held with absolute certainty within the context of classic checking compulsions, the diagnosis is Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder, With absent insight/delusional beliefs (NOT Schizophrenia or Delusional Disorder).",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: The 3 standardized insight tiers across OCRD are Good/Fair, Poor, and Absent/Delusional. If an obsessive belief is held with 100% delusional certainty, the diagnosis remains OCD with absent insight/delusional beliefs—it is NOT coded as Schizophrenia or Delusional Disorder. Up to 30% of OCD patients have a lifetime tic history (Tic-related specifier). Tic-related OCD features early childhood onset, male predominance, and symmetry/ordering rituals.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Three-Tier Insight Hierarchy', 'icon' => '👁️', 'definition' => 'Standardized across OCRD: Good/Fair (recognizes doubt), Poor (probably true), Absent/Delusional (100% convinced).'],
            ['title' => 'Delusional OCD Within Spectrum', 'icon' => '🔮', 'definition' => 'Delusional conviction within obsessive-compulsive themes is classified under OCRD, preventing misdiagnosis as schizophrenia.'],
            ['title' => 'The Tic-Related Specifier', 'icon' => '⚡', 'definition' => 'Identifies a distinct neurobiological subtype with childhood onset, male predominance, and frontostriatal dopamine involvement.'],
            ['title' => 'Phenomenological Boundary Protection', 'icon' => '🛡️', 'definition' => 'Preserving appropriate access to high-dose SSRIs and ERP therapy by correctly classifying delusional OCRD presentations.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Insight Spectrum', 'definition' => 'The degree to which an individual recognizes the irrationality or excessive nature of their psychiatric beliefs and behaviors.'],
            ['term' => 'Overvalued Ideation', 'definition' => 'A strongly held belief that falls between normal insight and full delusion, characteristic of poor insight in OCD and BDD.'],
            ['term' => 'Absent Insight', 'definition' => 'Complete lack of awareness of the pathological nature of an obsessive conviction, held with absolute delusional certainty.'],
            ['term' => 'Tourettic OCD', 'definition' => 'A clinical hybrid presentation featuring overlapping motor tics and symmetry compulsions driven by somatic \'just right\' premonitory sensations.']
        ]
    ],
    7 => [
        'title' => 'Cortico-Striato-Thalamo-Cortical (CSTC) Circuits & Models',
        'slug' => 'cstc-circuits-neurobiological-models-ocrd',
        'description' => 'Orbitofrontal cortex (OFC), anterior cingulate cortex (ACC), caudate nucleus, habit loops, and neurosurgical targets.',
        'overview' => "The neurobiology of Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders is fundamentally localized to dysfunction within Cortico-Striato-Thalamo-Cortical (CSTC) neurocircuits. In healthy brain functioning, CSTC loops serve as a sophisticated gating and filtering system that regulates habit formation, motor execution, error detection, and cognitive flexibility. This circuit connects the orbitofrontal cortex (OFC) and anterior cingulate cortex (ACC) to the striatum (caudate nucleus), which projects through the globus pallidus and substantia nigra to the thalamus, which in turn loops back to modulate cortical activity.\n\nIn OCD, structural and functional neuroimaging consistently reveals hyperactivity within the OFC and ACC paired with impaired inhibitory gating in the caudate nucleus. The OFC is responsible for evaluating threat and emotional valence; hyperactivation here generates persistent, intrusive feelings of danger and contamination (obsessions). The ACC mediates error detection and conflict monitoring; hyperactivation generates the persistent 'error signal' that something is profoundly wrong or incomplete.\n\nUnder normal conditions, the caudate nucleus filters out these erroneous cortical alarm signals. In OCD, a failure of striatal inhibition allows the 'worry loop' to continuously cycle through the thalamus back to the cortex. Compulsive behaviors emerge as motor actions driven by the striatum to quell this unrestrained error signal, reinforcing pathological habit loops over time.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "While CSTC neuroimaging is an investigative rather than direct clinical diagnostic criterion, evaluating CSTC circuit mechanisms informs clinical staging, prognosis, and treatment selection across the OCRD spectrum.\n\nClinicians evaluate: (1) error-detection hypervigilance (inability to dismiss feelings of incompleteness); (2) behavioral rigidity and cognitive inflexibility; (3) responsiveness to high-dose SSRI serotonergic modulators (which desensitize OFC hyperactivity over 8-12 weeks); and (4) suitability for advanced neuromodulation (Deep Brain Stimulation or stereotactic anterior capsulotomy) in severe, refractory cases.",
        'specifiers' => "Documenting treatment refractoriness and suitability for procedural interventions (e.g., Transcranial Magnetic Stimulation [TMS] targeting the supplementary motor area and bilateral medial prefrontal cortex).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Evaluating CSTC dysfunction against secondary neurological basal ganglia conditions: PANDAS / PANS (Pediatric Autoimmune Neuropsychiatric Disorders Associated with Streptococcal Infections), Sydenham Chorea, and post-encephalitic basal ganglia lesions.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 30-year-old patient with severe, refractory OCD who has failed trials of four high-dose SSRIs, clomipramine, and intensive residential ERP. Functional neuroimaging reveals profound hyperactivity within the orbitofrontal cortex, anterior cingulate cortex, and head of the caudate nucleus.\n\nThe clinical multidisciplinary team determines that the patient suffers from severe CSTC circuit hyperactivity, establishing eligibility for FDA-cleared deep Transcranial Magnetic Stimulation (dTMS) targeting the anterior cingulate and medial prefrontal cortex to downregulate hyperactive error signaling.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: The central neurocircuit in OCD is the Cortico-Striato-Thalamo-Cortical (CSTC) loop. The Orbitofrontal Cortex (OFC) mediates threat appraisal; the Anterior Cingulate Cortex (ACC) mediates error detection (the 'something is wrong' feeling); the Caudate Nucleus fails to filter out these signals. PANDAS involves autoimmune antibodies targeting the caudate nucleus following group A strep infection. High-dose SSRIs require 8-12 weeks to desensitize hyperactive OFC-ACC loops.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The CSTC Neurocircuit Loop', 'icon' => '🧠', 'definition' => 'Orbitofrontal cortex (threat) -> Anterior cingulate (error) -> Striatum/Caudate (gating) -> Thalamus -> Cortex.'],
            ['title' => 'Anterior Cingulate Error Signaling', 'icon' => '🚨', 'definition' => 'Hyperactivity in the ACC generates continuous, unyielding sensations that a catastrophic mistake has occurred.'],
            ['title' => 'Caudate Gating Deficit', 'icon' => '🚪', 'definition' => 'Loss of striatal inhibitory filtering allows persistent cortical alarm signals to loop uncontrollably to the thalamus.'],
            ['title' => 'Neurosurgical & Neuromodulation Targets', 'icon' => '🎯', 'definition' => 'Deep brain stimulation (ventral capsule/striatum) and anterior capsulotomy interrupt hyperactive CSTC fiber tracts in refractory OCD.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Orbitofrontal Cortex (OFC)', 'definition' => 'A prefrontal brain region that evaluates the emotional significance and threat value of environmental stimuli, hyperactive in OCD.'],
            ['term' => 'Anterior Cingulate Cortex (ACC)', 'definition' => 'A frontal limbic structure that detects errors and monitors cognitive conflict, responsible for the \'something is wrong\' feeling in OCD.'],
            ['term' => 'Caudate Nucleus', 'definition' => 'A major component of the basal ganglia that normally filters and gates cortical signals before transmission to the thalamus.'],
            ['term' => 'PANDAS', 'definition' => 'Pediatric Autoimmune Neuropsychiatric Disorders Associated with Streptococcal Infections, presenting with abrupt, overnight onset of severe OCD and tics in children.']
        ]
    ],
    8 => [
        'title' => 'Differential Diagnosis: OCD vs. OCPD vs. Psychosis vs. Illness Anxiety',
        'slug' => 'differential-diagnosis-ocd-ocpd-psychosis-illness-anxiety',
        'description' => 'Ego-dystonic OCD vs. ego-syntonic OCPD, absent insight vs. schizophrenia, and Illness Anxiety Disorder boundaries.',
        'overview' => "The differential diagnosis of Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder requires distinguishing true obsessive-compulsive psychopathology from personality traits, somatic health preoccupations, and psychotic spectrum conditions. Because patients may use colloquial terms like 'obsessed' to describe hobbies or perfectionism, clinical rigor requires establishing whether presenting behaviors are driven by ego-dystonic neutralizing rituals or ego-syntonic personality traits.\n\nThe most crucial diagnostic boundary is the distinction between OCD and Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder (OCPD). Despite their similar names, they are fundamentally distinct clinical conditions. OCD is an Axis I psychiatric disorder characterized by true, intrusive, ego-dystonic obsessions and repetitive neutralizing compulsions that consume time (>1 hour/day) and cause acute distress. In contrast, OCPD is an enduring, pervasive personality pattern characterized by perfectionism, orderliness, rigidity, interpersonal control, and moral scrupulosity that is ego-syntonic—the individual views their perfectionism as desirable, superior, and appropriate.\n\nSimilarly, differentiating Illness Anxiety Disorder from Contamination OCD requires analyzing the core threat: in Illness Anxiety Disorder, the patient is preoccupied with having or acquiring a specific internal medical disease (e.g., cancer), whereas in Contamination OCD, the patient fears external contaminants (germs, chemicals) and engages in repetitive external decontaminating rituals.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Systematic Differential Checklist:\n\n1. OCD vs. OCPD: Are there true obsessions (intrusive unwanted thoughts) and true compulsions (rituals)? If YES -> OCD. Is the presentation an enduring personality pattern of perfectionism, stubbornness, and control WITHOUT obsessions/compulsions? If YES -> OCPD. (Note: A patient can be co-diagnosed with both if criteria for each are met).\n\n2. OCD vs. Schizophrenia: In OCD with absent insight, the belief is tied directly to compulsive neutralizing rituals and lacks formal thought disorder, hallucinations, or negative symptoms; in Schizophrenia, delusions lack compulsive rituals and feature formal thought disorder.\n\n3. OCD vs. Illness Anxiety Disorder: In Illness Anxiety, fear targets an internal disease (e.g., brain tumor) without washing rituals; in OCD, fear targets external contamination neutralized by repetitive cleaning.\n\n4. OCD vs. Major Depressive Rumination: Depressive rumination focuses on past failures, guilt, and sadness without compulsive motor rituals.",
        'specifiers' => "Diagnostic formulations specify whether patients exhibit comorbid conditions (e.g., OCD comorbid with OCPD; or OCD comorbid with Major Depressive Disorder) and record the mandatory Insight Specifier.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "OCD vs. Autism Spectrum Disorder: In ASD, repetitive routines and lining up objects are comforting, ego-syntonic, and part of restricted interests; in OCD, compulsions are performed under duress to neutralize anxiety.\n\nOCD vs. Phobic Avoidance: In Phobia, avoidance is simple and lacks complex neutralizing motor or mental rituals; in OCD, avoidance is accompanied by elaborate cleaning, checking, or counting rituals.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 45-year-old manager referred by HR for interpersonal friction. The manager insists that employees format spreadsheets with exact margins, refuses to delegate tasks because 'others will not do them correctly', works 80 hours weekly while neglecting family, and hoards worn-out office pens.\n\nThe manager proudly asserts that their exacting standards are essential for workplace perfection and feels zero distress about their routines, complaining only that coworkers are sloppy and lazy. The manager reports NO intrusive thoughts, no washing rituals, and no fear of catastrophe. The ego-syntonic perfectionism, rigidity, and control confirm Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder (OCPD), ruling out OCD.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: OCD is ego-dystonic (intrusive obsessions and compulsions that the patient dislikes); OCPD is ego-syntonic (perfectionism and rigidity that the patient views as correct and superior). OCPD does NOT have true obsessions and compulsions. OCD and OCPD can be co-diagnosed if criteria for both are met. OCD with absent insight is distinguished from Schizophrenia by the presence of compulsive rituals and absence of hallucinations/thought disorder. Illness Anxiety targets internal disease; OCD targets external contamination.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Ego-Dystonic vs. Ego-Syntonic Axiom', 'icon' => '⚖️', 'definition' => 'OCD symptoms are experienced as alien and distressing; OCPD perfectionism is experienced as appropriate and desirable.'],
            ['title' => 'Absence of Rituals in OCPD', 'icon' => '🚫', 'definition' => 'OCPD features rigid character traits (orderliness, stubbornness) but lacks true intrusive obsessions and neutralizing compulsions.'],
            ['title' => 'Comorbid Co-Diagnosis Principle', 'icon' => '🤝', 'definition' => 'A patient who exhibits both intrusive compulsions and a pervasive perfectionistic personality style can be diagnosed with both OCD and OCPD.'],
            ['title' => 'Internal vs. External Contamination Boundary', 'icon' => '🔬', 'definition' => 'Illness Anxiety fears internal disease without cleaning rituals; OCD fears external contaminants neutralized by washing.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder (OCPD)', 'definition' => 'A Cluster C personality disorder characterized by a pervasive preoccupation with orderliness, perfectionism, mental and interpersonal control, at the expense of flexibility and efficiency.'],
            ['term' => 'Ego-Syntonic Perfectionism', 'definition' => 'A characterological belief that one\'s exacting standards, rigid rules, and high demands are rational, correct, and superior to those of others.'],
            ['term' => 'Depressive Rumination', 'definition' => 'Repetitive, passive thinking focused on the causes, meanings, and consequences of one\'s depressive symptoms without active problem-solving.'],
            ['term' => 'Delegation Reluctance', 'definition' => 'A core diagnostic criterion of OCPD wherein an individual refuses to delegate tasks to others unless they submit exactly to his or her way of doing things.']
        ]
    ],
    9 => [
        'title' => 'Educational Case Vignettes: Evaluating Compulsive Patterns',
        'slug' => 'educational-case-vignettes-ocrd',
        'description' => 'Clinical case formulations, Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale (Y-BOCS), differential decision trees, and board pearls.',
        'overview' => "Clinical competency in Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders requires the systematic integration of structured clinical interviews, standardized psychometric rating scales, and longitudinal behavioral observation. The gold-standard clinical instrument for quantifying OCD severity is the Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale (Y-BOCS), which independently measures time spent, interference, distress, resistance, and degree of control across both obsessions and compulsions.\n\nFormulating OCRD cases demands precise differentiation between obsessive-compulsive phenomena and competing clinical entities, such as tic disorders, body dysmorphic concerns, hoarding behaviors, and impulse-control disorders. Clinicians must determine whether repetitive motor acts represent true compulsions (performed to neutralize obsessional anxiety) or impulsivity (performed for immediate sensory pleasure or gratification).\n\nThis educational module analyzes multi-faceted clinical vignettes simulating complex outpatient presentations and board examination stems, demonstrating how to systematically apply DSM-5 criteria, assign insight specifiers, and establish evidence-based treatment plans.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Case Formulation Methodology:\n1. Step 1: Identify Repetitive Behaviors. Are behaviors performed to neutralize intrusive anxiety (OCD/BDD) or are they driven by tactile pleasure/gratification (Impulse control, BFRBs)?\n2. Step 2: Evaluate Primary Focus. Is focus on physical flaws (BDD), discarding possessions (Hoarding), hair/skin (Trichotillomania/Excoriation), or intrusive obsessions (OCD)?\n3. Step 3: Establish Clinical Severity via Y-BOCS. Quantify time spent (>1 hour/day), distress, and functional interference.\n4. Step 4: Mandatory Specifiers. Assign Insight Specifier (Good/Fair, Poor, Absent/Delusional) and Tic Specifier.",
        'specifiers' => "Diagnostic formulations must record primary diagnoses, insight tiers, tic status, and Y-BOCS baseline scores.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Case reviews systematically contrast: OCD vs. BDD; OCD vs. Hoarding Disorder; Trichotillomania vs. Excoriation; and OCD with Delusional Beliefs vs. Psychotic Disorder.",
        'clinical_example' => "Educational Case Vignette: A 28-year-old accountant presents with profound distress regarding nighttime home security. Every night, the accountant checks all window latches, door deadbolts, and appliance switches in an exact sequence of 16 repetitions, spending 2.5 hours per night.\n\nIf the accountant loses count, they experience overwhelming terror that intruders will murder their family, forcing them to restart the entire sequence from step one. The accountant recognizes that the fear is highly exaggerated (fair insight) but feels powerless to stop. The Y-BOCS score is 28 (Severe OCD). The clinical formulation confirms Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder, With good or fair insight, Harm/Checking dimension, directing treatment toward Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP) and high-dose SSRI therapy.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: The Y-BOCS is the gold-standard psychometric scale for measuring OCD symptom severity. Y-BOCS scores range from 0 to 40; scores >= 24 indicate severe OCD. Always document the mandatory Insight Specifier (Good/Fair, Poor, Absent/Delusional). Compulsions reduce anxiety; impulsive behaviors provide initial pleasure. First-line evidence-based treatments are Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP) and high-dose SSRIs.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Y-BOCS Psychometric Standard', 'icon' => '📊', 'definition' => 'The Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale measures time, interference, distress, resistance, and control (0-40 scale).'],
            ['title' => 'Compulsive vs. Impulsive Distinction', 'icon' => '⚖️', 'definition' => 'Compulsions neutralize dysphoria and anxiety; impulsive acts seek immediate pleasure, arousal, or gratification.'],
            ['title' => 'Standardized Insight Documentation', 'icon' => '👁️', 'definition' => 'Mandatory assignment of Good/Fair, Poor, or Absent/Delusional insight on every OCRD clinical formulation.'],
            ['title' => 'ERP First-Line Efficacy', 'icon' => '🎯', 'definition' => 'Exposure and Response Prevention demonstrates superior long-term effect sizes compared to pharmacotherapy alone.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Y-BOCS', 'definition' => 'Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale; a clinician-rated 10-item instrument assessing the severity of obsessions and compulsions.'],
            ['term' => 'Compulsive Ritual', 'definition' => 'A stereotyped, repetitive motor behavior or mental act performed according to rigid rules to alleviate obsessional anxiety.'],
            ['term' => 'Resistance Score', 'definition' => 'A subscale of the Y-BOCS measuring how actively and vigorously the patient attempts to resist and combat their obsessive-compulsive symptoms.'],
            ['term' => 'Behavioral Homework', 'definition' => 'Structured, therapist-assigned in vivo exposure exercises performed by the patient in their home environment between therapy sessions.']
        ]
    ],
    10 => [
        'title' => 'Chapter Mastery & Comprehensive Board Review',
        'slug' => 'chapter-mastery-board-review-ocrd',
        'description' => 'Comprehensive synthesis of Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders, duration thresholds, diagnostic hierarchies, and high-yield board pearls.',
        'overview' => "This capstone unit provides a comprehensive synthesis of the Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders (OCRD) chapter under DSM-5 criteria, consolidating core diagnostic distinctions, duration thresholds, and high-yield board pearls for professional licensure examinations. OCRD conditions represent highly tested topics across clinical examinations, demanding exact command over diagnostic criteria, specifiers, and differential boundaries.\n\nMastery requires commanding the clinical distinctions separating Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder, Body Dysmorphic Disorder, Hoarding Disorder, Trichotillomania, Excoriation Disorder, and OCPD. Furthermore, candidates must navigate DSM-5 structural refinements, including the creation of the standalone OCRD chapter, the mandatory uniform Insight Specifiers (Good/Fair, Poor, Absent/Delusional), the Tic-related specifier, and the elimination of the tension-relief requirement in BFRBs.\n\nBy synthesizing clinical vignettes, diagnostic decision matrices, and evidence-based neurobiological mechanisms, this unit prepares students and clinicians for board examination excellence and compassionate clinical practice.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Mastery Summary of Core OCRD Diagnostic Rules:\n\n1. Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder: Presence of obsessions, compulsions, or both; time-consuming (> 1 hour per day) or causing clinically significant distress/impairment. Mandatory insight specifier (Good/Fair, Poor, Absent/Delusional); Tic specifier.\n\n2. Body Dysmorphic Disorder: Preoccupation with perceived physical defects that are not observable or appear only slight to others; repetitive behaviors (mirror checking, grooming) or mental acts; 'With muscle dysmorphia' specifier; cosmetic surgery is contraindicated.\n\n3. Hoarding Disorder: Persistent difficulty discarding possessions regardless of value; perceived need to save; congestion/clutter of active living spaces compromising intended use; 'With excessive acquisition' specifier (80-90%).\n\n4. Trichotillomania: Recurrent hair pulling resulting in hair loss; repeated attempts to decrease/stop.\n\n5. Excoriation Disorder: Recurrent skin picking resulting in lesions; repeated attempts to decrease/stop.\n\n6. OCPD: Pervasive perfectionism, orderliness, and control; ego-syntonic; NO true obsessions or compulsions.",
        'specifiers' => "Review of essential specifiers: Standardized Insight Specifiers (Good/Fair, Poor, Absent/Delusional); 'Tic-related' in OCD; 'With muscle dysmorphia' in BDD; 'With excessive acquisition' in Hoarding Disorder.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Key Board Differential Pearls:\n• Hand washing for 3 hours daily to neutralize germ fears -> OCD (> 1 hr/day).\n• Checking mirrors for 4 hours daily believing nose is grotesque -> BDD (slight/unnoticeable flaw).\n• Accumulating stacks of paper filling kitchen and bedroom -> Hoarding Disorder (compromises active living areas).\n• Pulling hair while studying with repeated failed attempts to stop -> Trichotillomania.\n• Perfectionistic manager with rigid spreadsheet rules and no rituals -> OCPD (ego-syntonic).\n• 100% convinced house will burn down without checking -> OCD, With Absent Insight/Delusional Beliefs (NOT Schizophrenia).\n• Compulsive symmetry with childhood motor tics -> OCD, Tic-related.",
        'clinical_example' => "A comprehensive clinical review scenario: A board examination vignette describes a 24-year-old male who spends three hours per day examining minor blemishes on his cheeks in magnifying mirrors, using foundation makeup to camouflage them, and seeking consultations with four plastic surgeons. The plastic surgeons confirm that his skin has only normal, minor pores that are unnoticeable to casual observers.\n\nBoard Exam Question Formulation: What is the diagnosis? The candidate recognizes that preoccupation with unobservable physical flaws, paired with compulsive mirror checking and camouflaging, confirms Body Dysmorphic Disorder. Because the patient is 100% convinced that his face is hideous and that surgeons are lying to comfort him, the candidate correctly adds the specifier: With absent insight/delusional beliefs.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board pearls summary: OCRD is an independent chapter in DSM-5. OCD requires >1 hour/day. BDD requires perceived flaws that are unnoticeable to others; cosmetic surgery does not help. Hoarding compromises active living spaces; saving is ego-syntonic. Trichotillomania and Excoriation require repeated attempts to stop. Standardized insight specifiers: Good/Fair, Poor, Absent/Delusional. Delusional OCD stays in OCRD. OCD is ego-dystonic; OCPD is ego-syntonic.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'OCRD Diagnostic Decision Matrix', 'icon' => '🏛️', 'definition' => 'Comprehensive command of duration thresholds, symptom counts, and severity rules across all obsessive-compulsive spectrum disorders.'],
            ['title' => 'The Uniform Insight Architecture', 'icon' => '👁️', 'definition' => 'Standardized 3-tier insight specifier applied systematically across OCD, BDD, and Hoarding Disorder.'],
            ['title' => 'Compulsive vs. Characterological Distinction', 'icon' => '⚖️', 'definition' => 'Differentiating ego-dystonic OCD rituals from ego-syntonic OCPD personality perfectionism.'],
            ['title' => 'First-Line Somatic & Behavioral Protocols', 'icon' => '💊', 'definition' => 'Commanding Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP) and high-dose SSRI dosing regimens for OCRD.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'CSTC Circuitry', 'definition' => 'The Cortico-Striato-Thalamo-Cortical neural pathway connecting the orbitofrontal cortex, anterior cingulate, and caudate nucleus, central to OCRD neurobiology.'],
            ['term' => 'Ego-Dystonic Neutralization', 'definition' => 'The execution of compulsive behaviors designed to temporarily alleviate the intense anxiety triggered by an intrusive, unwanted obsession.'],
            ['term' => 'Active Living Compromise', 'definition' => 'The diagnostic benchmark in Hoarding Disorder wherein clutter renders rooms unusable for cooking, sleeping, or personal hygiene.'],
            ['term' => 'Habit Reversal Protocol', 'definition' => 'The evidence-based behavioral treatment for Body-Focused Repetitive Behaviors combining awareness training and competing motor responses.']
        ]
    ]
];
